Core Principle
The /brain folder is the single source of truth for all project knowledge. Code may change, but brain remembers everything—decisions, tests, bugs, experiments, failures, and evolution. After a year away, Cursor reads brain and instantly has full context, as if never interrupted.

📁 Brain Structure
text
/brain
  ├── INDEX.md (auto-updated master list of all features & docs)
  ├── /features
  │   ├── /[feature-name]
  │   │   ├── README.md (what, why, current status)
  │   │   ├── journal.md (chronological work log)
  │   │   ├── tests.md (all test cases, coverage, edge cases)
  │   │   ├── bugs.md (active & resolved bugs, patterns)
  │   │   └── decisions.md (key choices & rationale)
  ├── /architecture
  │   ├── system-overview.md
  │   ├── tech-stack.md
  │   ├── patterns-and-conventions.md
  │   └── dependencies.md
  ├── /ui-ux
  │   ├── design-system.md
  │   ├── user-feedback.md
  │   ├── accessibility.md
  │   └── /design-assets
  │       └── /[feature-name] (screenshots, Figma exports, flows)
  ├── /project-memory
  │   ├── timeline.md (chronological master log with emoji markers)
  │   ├── state-of-app.md (monthly snapshot: progress, metrics, priorities)
  │   ├── open-questions.md (unresolved decisions & investigations)
  │   ├── rejected-ideas.md (what we didn't do & why)
  │   └── failed-experiments.md (dead ends preserved as lessons)
  ├── /performance
  │   ├── baselines.md (load times, memory, fps benchmarks)
  │   └── optimization-log.md (improvements & regressions)
  └── /archives
      └── /[year] (historical context no longer immediately relevant)
🎯 Mandatory Rules for Cursor
1. Every Feature Gets a Brain Folder
When starting any new feature, Cursor immediately creates /brain/features/[feature-name]/ with all five template files (README, journal, tests, bugs, decisions).

2. Bidirectional Linking
Code → Brain: Every source file has a header comment pointing to its brain doc

javascript
/**
 * Feature: Authentication Screen
 * Brain: /brain/features/authentication/README.md
 * Status: Stable ✅ | Last Updated: 2025-10-24
 */
Brain → Code: Every brain doc links to actual source files and test files

3. Nothing Exists Outside Brain
If a decision, test, bug, or idea isn't documented in brain, it doesn't exist. Brain is not optional—it's the project's memory.

4. Update Brain & Code in Same Commit
Never commit code changes without updating relevant brain docs. Every commit must include both.

5. Definition of Done Checklist
Every task is complete only when:

✅ Code written and working

✅ Tests written and passing (documented in tests.md)

✅ Brain docs updated (journal, decisions, tests, bugs)

✅ Cross-references added between brain and code

✅ Entry added to /brain/project-memory/timeline.md

✅ INDEX.md auto-updated

✅ Commit with descriptive message referencing brain docs

6. Test Everything, Document Everything
Every feature must have:

Unit tests (component/function level)

Integration tests (feature interaction)

E2E tests (full user flows)

Bug tests (reproduce & verify fixes)

All test results recorded in /brain/features/[feature]/tests.md with:

Status (pass/fail/pending)

Coverage percentage

Edge cases considered

Untested scenarios

Performance benchmarks

7. Bug Tracking in Brain
All bugs logged in /brain/features/[feature]/bugs.md:

🔥 Active bugs (severity, steps to reproduce, workaround)

✅ Resolved bugs (fix details, commit reference)

📊 Bug patterns (recurring issues, root causes, prevention strategies)

8. Re-Entry Protocol
Before making any changes after time away (hours, days, months, years), Cursor must complete this checklist:

text
🔄 Cursor Re-Entry Checklist

1. ✅ Read /brain/project-memory/timeline.md (last 3 months)
2. ✅ Read /brain/project-memory/state-of-app.md (latest snapshot)
3. ✅ Review /brain/project-memory/open-questions.md
4. ✅ Check /brain/architecture/system-overview.md
5. ✅ Review specific feature's brain folder
6. ✅ Check /brain/INDEX.md for new features
7. ✅ Run all existing tests to verify current state
8. ✅ Check for dependency updates or breaking changes

✅ Only after completing this may Cursor begin work.
9. Monthly State Snapshot
On the first commit of each month, Cursor updates /brain/project-memory/state-of-app.md with:

Overall progress (X/Y features complete, %)

Test coverage %

Bug count by severity

Performance metrics vs baselines

Major changes this month

Open questions

Next month priorities

10. Preserve All Context—Even Failures
Rejected ideas → /brain/project-memory/rejected-ideas.md

Failed experiments → /brain/project-memory/failed-experiments.md

Open questions → /brain/project-memory/open-questions.md

This prevents repeating past mistakes or debates.

11. Use Structured Markdown + YAML Frontmatter
Every brain doc uses YAML frontmatter for machine parsing:

text
---
feature: authentication
status: stable | in-progress | experimental | deprecated
priority: critical | high | medium | low
owner: cursor
last_updated: 2025-10-24
tags: [security, user-flow, mobile]
---

# Feature Name
...
This makes brain both human-readable and programmatically queryable.

12. Emoji Markers for Quick Scanning
Use consistent emoji in timeline and journals:

🎯 New feature started

✅ Feature completed

🧪 Tests added/updated

🐛 Bug found

🔥 Critical issue

🎨 UI/UX change

📊 Performance improvement

❓ Open question

💡 New idea

🗑️ Code removed/deprecated

13. Cross-Reference Liberally
Every brain doc should link to related docs. If /brain/features/authentication/decisions.md references a pattern from /brain/architecture/patterns-and-conventions.md, link it. This creates a knowledge web.

14. Archive, Don't Delete
When context becomes outdated, move it to /brain/archives/[year]/ rather than deleting. History is preserved.

15. Auto-Update INDEX.md
After every new feature or major brain update, regenerate /brain/INDEX.md with:

List of all features

One-line description

Status

Last updated date

This is Cursor's entry point for quick discovery.

